(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_nuclei=[{id:"neet-2026-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Nuclear size and density",q:`An unknown nucleus has a nuclear density of 2.29\xD710\xB9\u2077 kg/m\xB3 and mass of 19.926\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg.. Its mass number A is approximately:
(Take R\u2080 = 1.2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075 m, 4\u03C0 = 12.56)`,o:["12","19","20","16"],c:0,s:`Given: \u03C1 = 2.29\xD710\xB9\u2077 kg/m\xB3, mass m = 19.926\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg, R\u2080 = 1.2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075 m and R = R\u2080A^(1/3).
Volume = Mass/Density, so (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3 = M/\u03C1.
(4/3)\u03C0[R\u2080A^(1/3)]\xB3 = M/\u03C1.
(4/3)\u03C0R\u2080\xB3A = M/\u03C1.
A = (M/\u03C1) \xD7 3/(4\u03C0 R\u2080\xB3).
A = (19.926\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 3)/(2.29\xD710\xB9\u2077 \xD7 12.56 \xD7 (1.2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075)\xB3) \u2248 12.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q036",pn:36,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Q value of nuclear reaction",q:`Consider the following nuclear reaction
\xB2\xB3\u2078U \u2192 \xB2\xB3\u2074Th + \u2074He
Take masses of \xB2\xB3\u2078U, \xB2\xB3\u2074Th and \u2074He as 238.050 u, 234.043 u and 4.003 u, respectively. The Q value for the reaction, in keV, is:
[Given: 1 u = 931.5 MeV c\u207B\xB2]`,o:["3730","3736","3740","3726"],c:3,s:`Q-value of a nuclear reaction is Q = \u0394mc\xB2.
Mass defect \u0394m = m(reactants) \u2212 m(products).
\u0394m = 238.050 \u2212 (234.043 + 4.003) = 0.004 u.
Q = 0.004 \xD7 931.5 MeV = 3.726 MeV = 3726 keV.`},{id:"neet-2026-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Nuclear size and mass defect",q:`Four statements are given (A is mass number):
A. The volume of a nucleus is proportional to A^(1/3).
B. The volume of a nucleus is proportional to A.
C. The difference in mass of an atom and its nucleus is called the mass defect.
D. The difference in mass of a nucleus and its constituents is called the mass defect.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and D are true, but A and C are false","A and D are true, but B and C are false","A and C are true, but B and D are false","B and C are true, but A and D are false"],c:0,s:`Size of nucleus: r = r\u2080A^(1/3).
Cubing: r\xB3 = r\u2080\xB3\xB7A.
Volume V = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3, so V \u221D A.
Thus A is wrong and B is correct.
The difference between the actual mass of the nucleus and its constituents is the mass defect.
So C is wrong and D is correct.`},{id:"neet-2024-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Alpha, beta, positron decay",q:`\xB2\u2079\u2070\u2088\u2082X \u2192(\u03B1) Y \u2192(e\u207A) Z \u2192(\u03B2\u207B) P \u2192(e\u207B) Q
In the nuclear emission stated above, the mass number and atomic number of the product Q respectively, are:`,o:["288, 82","286, 81","280, 81","286, 80"],c:1,s:`\u03B1 emission lowers A by 4 and Z by 2: \xB2\u2079\u2070\u2088\u2082X \u2192 \xB2\u2078\u2076\u2088\u2080Y.
Positron (e\u207A) emission keeps A and lowers Z by 1: \xB2\u2078\u2076\u2087\u2089Z.
\u03B2\u207B emission keeps A and raises Z by 1: \xB2\u2078\u2076\u2088\u2080P.
Electron (e\u207B) emission again raises Z by 1: \xB2\u2078\u2076\u2088\u2081Q.
So A = 286 and Z = 81.`},{id:"neet-2023-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Radioactive decay and half-life",q:"The half life of a radioactive substance is 20 minutes. In how much time, the activity of substance drops to (1/16)th of its of its initial value?",o:["80 minutes","20 minutes","40 minutes","60 minutes"],c:0,s:`After n half-lives, activity A = A\u2080/2\u207F.
Given A/A\u2080 = 1/16 = 1/2\u2074, so n = 4.
n = t/T\xBD, so t = 4 \xD7 T\xBD = 4 \xD7 20.
t = 80 minutes.`},{id:"neet-2022-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Beta plus decay",q:`In the given nuclear reaction, the element X is
\xB2\xB2\u2081\u2081Na \u2192 X + e\u207A + \u03BD`,o:["\xB2\xB3\u2081\u2080Ne","\xB2\xB2\u2081\u2080Ne","\xB2\xB2\u2081\u2082Mg","\xB2\xB3\u2081\u2081Na"],c:1,s:`Write the reaction as \xB2\xB2\u2081\u2081Na \u2192 \u1D2CZX + \u2070\u208A\u2081e + \u03BD.
Charge conservation: 11 = Z + 1, so Z = 10, which is Ne.
Mass number: 22 = A + 0, so A = 22.
X is \xB2\xB2\u2081\u2080Ne.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q027",pn:27,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Radioactive decay law",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: The law of radioactive decay states that the number of nuclei undergoing the decay per unit time is inversely proportional to the total number of nuclei in the sample.
Statement II: The half life of a radionuclide is the sum of the life time of all nuclei, divided by the initial concentration of the nuclei at the t = 0.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],c:2,s:`The decay law says \u2212dN/dt \u221D N, so the rate is directly proportional to N, not inversely. Statement I is wrong.
Half life is the time in which half of the active nuclei decay. Statement II describes mean life, not half life, so it is wrong too.`},{id:"neet-2022-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Nuclear size and mass number",q:"A nucleus of mass number 189 splits into two nuclei having mass number 125 and 64. The ratio of radius of two daughter nuclei respectively is",o:["4 : 5","5 : 4","25 : 16","1 : 1"],c:1,s:`Nuclear radius varies as R = R\u2080A^(1/3).
R\u2081/R\u2082 = (125/64)^(1/3).
This is 5/4, so the ratio is 5 : 4.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q040",pn:40,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Decay of two samples",q:"At any instant, two elements X\u2081 and X\u2082 have same number of radioactive atoms. If the decay constant of X\u2081 and X\u2082 are 10\u03BB and \u03BB respectively, then the time when the ratio of their atoms becomes 1/e respectively will be:",o:["1/(5\u03BB)","1/(11\u03BB)","1/(9\u03BB)","1/(6\u03BB)"],c:2,s:`N_x = N\u2080e^(\u2212\u03BB\u2093t) and N_y = N\u2080e^(\u2212\u03BB\u1D67t).
N_x/N_y = e^(\u2212(\u03BB\u2093\u2212\u03BB\u1D67)t) = 1/e = e\u207B\xB9.
So (\u03BB\u2093 \u2212 \u03BB\u1D67)t = 1.
t = 1/(10\u03BB \u2212 \u03BB) = 1/(9\u03BB).`},{id:"neet-2021-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Radioactive decay",q:"A radioactive nucleus \u1D2C_Z X undergoes spontaneous decay in the sequence \u1D2C_Z X \u2192 _{Z\u22121}B \u2192 _{Z\u22123}C \u2192 _{Z\u22122}D, where Z is the atomic number of element X. The possible decay particles in the sequence are",o:["\u03B2\u207B, \u03B1, \u03B2\u207A","\u03B1, \u03B2\u207B, \u03B2\u207A","\u03B1, \u03B2\u207A, \u03B2\u207B","\u03B2\u207A, \u03B1, \u03B2\u207B"],c:3,s:`Z \u2192 Z\u20131: atomic number falls by 1, so \u03B2\u207A.
Z\u20131 \u2192 Z\u20133: falls by 2, so \u03B1.
Z\u20133 \u2192 Z\u20132: rises by 1, so \u03B2\u207B.
Order: \u03B2\u207A, \u03B1, \u03B2\u207B.`},{id:"neet-2021-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Half-life",q:"The half-life of a radioactive nuclide is 100 hours. The fraction of original activity that will remain after 150 hours would be -",o:["2/(3\u221A2)","1/2","1/(2\u221A2)","2/3"],c:2,s:`t/T\xBD = 150/100 = 3/2.
A/A\u2080 = (1/2)^(3/2) = 1/(2\u221A2).`},{id:"neet-2021-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Binding energy",q:"A nucleus with mass number 240 breaks into two fragments each of mass number 120, the binding energy per nucleon of unfragmented nuclei is 7.6 MeV while that of fragments is 8.5 MeV. The total gain in the Binding Energy in the process is",o:["216 MeV","0.9 MeV","9.4 MeV","804 MeV"],c:0,s:`Binding energy before = 240 \xD7 7.6 = 1824 MeV.
Binding energy after = 240 \xD7 8.5 = 2040 MeV.
Gain = 2040 \u2013 1824 = 216 MeV.`},{id:"neet-2020-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Mass-energy relation",q:"The energy equivalent of 0.5 g of a substance is :",o:["4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J","1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J","0.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J","4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J"],c:0,s:`E = mc\xB2.
m = 0.5 g = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg, c\xB2 = 9 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 m\xB2/s\xB2.
E = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 = 4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J.
Note: Options (1) and (4) print the same value 4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J; the printed solution has a typing error (10\u207B\xB3) but its working gives 4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J. Printed key 1 kept.`},{id:"neet-2020-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Nuclear fission",q:"When a uranium isotope \xB2\xB3\u2075\u2089\u2082U is bombarded with a neutron, it generates \u2078\u2079\u2083\u2086Kr, three neutrons and :",o:["\u2079\xB9\u2084\u2080Zr","\xB9\u2070\xB9\u2083\u2086Kr","\xB9\u2070\xB3\u2083\u2086Kr","\xB9\u2074\u2074\u2085\u2086Ba"],c:3,s:`Reaction: \xB2\xB3\u2075\u2089\u2082U + \xB9\u2080n \u2192 \u2078\u2079\u2083\u2086Kr + X + 3 \xB9\u2080n.
Mass number: 235 + 1 = 89 + A + 3, so A = 144.
Charge: 92 = 36 + Z, so Z = 56.
X is \xB9\u2074\u2074\u2085\u2086Ba.`},{id:"neet-2019-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Alpha particle",q:"\u03B1-particle consists of :",o:["2 electrons and 4 protons only","2 protons only","2 protons and 2 neutrons only","2 electrons, 2 protons and 2 neutrons"],c:2,s:`An \u03B1-particle is the nucleus of a helium atom.
It has 2 protons and 2 neutrons and no electrons.`}]})();
